{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the complete collections note, primary 5c69660e656c81a415c56484f1b6bd6e6f1ab5ff at exact 5b579511c29fb70e488c7cc4370640154c44f41f, under 34cf52b3 / 6430ffd4 and review 106bd3ca / promise 10d431c5. I read all 47,931 UTF-8 bytes / 630 content lines, SHA256 4179cca075e3c9ff2ed83ae7519eea98e23228c71375d7f636f054ea9f65038d, and vetted two complete independent readings against the cited contracts and DAP examples.\n\nThe note supplies the requested three-mechanism table, ten numbered walkthroughs, four revocation alternatives, 41 traced requirements, exclusions and Source/Judgement/Untested labels. Preserve Hugh's title, opening engagement model and full functional ambition. The following corrections are needed before the proposed requirements support adoption.\n\n1. P1 — Make the revocation input replayable, fresh and authorized (132–137, 433–460, REQ-25–31). Recording a list version does not give a standalone replayer the state used for a successful admission, when no revocation object was found. Define the retained immutable state or bounded completeness/non-membership evidence, its trusted completeness and freshness boundary, cache age, rollback protection, and the final admission check. An old correctly signed cached state can omit an accepted revocation. Preserve the one-minute requirement and independent replay rather than making replay depend on a mutable external service. A pinned signature identifies an issuer but does not authorize every target: define which principal/key/delegation and cell scope each firm may revoke. Two firms in W6/REQ-8 must not let firm A remove customer or firm B authority. W8 includes compromise of the firm's own member signing key (238, 335–336), but D only checks delegated/mandated admissions. Decide whether the firm pin can sign directly or extend the fast-stop coverage to affected direct keys; keeping unrelated direct members available is a separate requirement. Specify the applicable recovery/pin rules with existing G2 owner d50ce26d. Distinguishing design cases: stale list state, absent revocation evidence, unrelated issuer/target, and the affected direct-key route. These are missing proposed rules, not reproduced runtime vulnerabilities.\n\n2. P2 — Reconcile targeted audit work and outage behavior (448–453, REQ-27, REQ-32–35). Issued mandates or assignments do not prove a key acted in a cell. Targeting every assigned cell can wake cells that REQ-27 promises do no work. State how actual affected activity/evidence identifies recipients, including incomplete knowledge and retries, or explicitly revise that guarantee; do not use assignments as proof of activity. Also, mandate expiry bounds stale authority, not the duration of a list outage. REQ-35 refuses while D is unavailable, potentially beyond the mandate lifetime. Correct “at most the mandate lifetime”; any mandate-only fallback needs an explicit choice and a reconciled timeliness claim. Retain ordinary local revocation and the four-alternative comparison.\n\n3. P1 — Preserve the sale's authoritative decision and state the privacy limits of the alternative (W7:319–330, REQ-38). Two independent bidder cells can each accept a different winning decision. Signing one chosen object into both does not establish a unique winner or settle delayed/conflicting copies. Name the authoritative decision record and show duplicate acceptance, a conflicting copy and a delayed/refused carry. This may be a single decision Room/record with privacy-preserving carries, without any cross-Room admission read. The cited DAP sale orders two accepts in one sale: the first decides it and the second is ineffective. It also refuses Alice's requested disclosure of private price/counter/inspection records to Dana. “What Alice chooses to share” does not preserve that contract. State precisely which disclosure rules the multi-Room alternative preserves and which remain unsupported. Keep per-audience visibility G7 undecided; do not claim the source agreement/privacy properties are reproduced merely by attribution and copies. Source comparison: DAP main inspected at 9d738e2e71b84ec85cd321bd4d87623ea3fad355, sale-as-experienced:213–236,473–499.\n\n4. P1 — Authorize publication of reporting facts at the source cell (W10:377–387, REQ-18–24). A grouping roster authorizes its own aggregate, not disclosure from its constituent cells. KPI expressions can encode private content, and credential checks on drill-in do not authorize facts already exported and retained. Define the source-cell-authorized fields, audience and export/read permission, plus retention and access-change behavior. Include a grouping reader who lacks cell permission and an expression that attempts to expose private data. Authorized copied facts cannot be made unread retroactively by implication. Apply appropriate permission and bounded-query rules to registry coordinates as well. Keep reporting copies separate from authority and retain the choice of facts-store technology; “facts, never content” alone does not enforce privacy.\n\n5. P2 — Preserve work without preserving stale execution authority (W4:243, REQ-6). Distinguish continuity of the firm's undertaking, lane history and authorized saved work from a live environment, token or former representative's controls. Cross-reference adopted 005/C1/C3 requirements for current authority, explicit replacement access, epoch rebinding, stale callback fencing and unresolved external-effect duties. The initial hosted-work model has no in-place owner transfer; the firm-specific handover must identify how it fits that boundary or what separately reviewed authority it needs. Keep this with d50ce26d, not a new implementation lane. Also correct W4.5–6: R-ADM-3(c) join has no delegation, while case (b) does not require the delegated agent to join and cannot delegate roster authority. Choose a coherent customer/member/delegated-agent path and state who is attributed, rather than citing both incompatible paths as today's rule.\n\n6. P2 — Trace changing standing and usable agency, not only provenance (W5, REQ-15–17,109–114). DAP's account keeps captured bytes unchanged with claimed source/time, records interpretation separately with its author, and changes/display standing after cancellation, correction, retraction or conflicting sources. An authentic old confirmation must not appear as the current arrangement. Trace those cases to the personal pack and its displayed result, keeping shared decision guards and private personal evidence distinct. Symmetric identity/delegation mechanics are a design boundary and a hypothesis about the unsupported customer's advantage; they do not prove usable agency “by construction.” Retain the source outcomes of coherent standing, usable next steps and needs met/unmet/human-supplied. Preserve N2's complete addressed-work and handover/cancellation lifecycle as separately owed; copying/provenance is not its replacement. No pilot or first-Jam gate follows from this correction.\n\n7. P3 — Make source and delivery claims precise (W1:163–182; W9; Sources). W1 records a signed checker check yet says the service never writes into the Room; describe locally judged submissions rather than privileged control. Git push is distinct from a signed Room act. Label the pi evidence as its bounded local workerd, pinned-source, caller-retried/simulated-push spike, with autonomous production C3 and attention/wake delivery still owed. Keep the dated bd520fb9 protocol baseline distinct from landed main e6e67828 and the reviewed, unlanded declared-acts candidate. W9's external-catalogue attention must identify the existing C3/007/008 runtime/input owner and local current-authority admission, without implying that a cell evaluator reads another Room. Pin the DAP and branch-note source versions used. Reconcile the workflow/requirement labels with REQ-41 so unmeasured admission cost, propagation, idle scale and runtime behavior receive their stated spikes before adoption, without turning old evidence or a planning request into production readiness.\n\nArchitecture: cell-local admission, pinned authority/pack versions and explicitly carried provenance are a useful scalable direction. The three control relations remain distinct. The corrections make the new external admission fact, single shared decision and copied reporting data explicit rather than hiding their authority boundaries.\n\nSecurity: issuer authentication, target authorization, freshness, direct-key coverage, export permission and replacement execution authority are independent obligations. Removal-only and facts-only wording does not discharge them. No implemented security boundary is approved here.\n\nSimplification: keep one cell per engagement/private relationship, one explicit authoritative decision where exclusivity is required, existing mandate/runtime/attention/lifecycle owners, and a small set of distinguishing counterexamples. Retain the full scope and exclusions; estimates affect sequencing, not functionality. This planning review commissions no new implementation, runs no application tests, changes no source and creates no deployment or first-Jam gate. Source integration remains owed under the original request.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5c69660e656c81a415c56484f1b6bd6e6f1ab5ff","binding":"assigned","head":"5b579511c29fb70e488c7cc4370640154c44f41f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:10d431c564a210062479d455d734b2e6606596e1\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6430ffd49f700c75594c4feb4ccb89561a5908bd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:67957c6a6be54c3abc73beb7b94528d0b60aa1e9","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}